    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A pressure washer for home use typically costs roughly $100 to $250 for a light-duty electric, $200 to $450 for a stronger electric, $300 to $600 for a homeowner gas machine, and $700 to $1,500 or more for a professional-grade gas unit. Running costs are small, from well under a dollar an hour for electric to a dollar or two an hour in fuel for gas, but plan on another $50 to $150 for the accessories most people end up buying. Renting usually runs roughly $50 to $120 a day, and hiring a pressure washing company commonly costs a few hundred dollars for a house or deck. All prices here are typical US ranges that vary by region and change over time, so treat them as a budgeting guide rather than a quote.
        </p>

        <div id="by-class" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Does Each Type of Pressure Washer Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure washers fall into four broad price classes, and the class matters more than the brand. Light-duty electric machines handle cars, patio furniture, grills and small patios. Mid-range electric machines can clean a deck, a driveway and most siding if you are patient. Homeowner gas machines clean big concrete areas and heavy grime much faster. Professional gas units are built to run for hours a day, every day, and most homeowners do not need one.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Class</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical rating</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical price range</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light-duty electric</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500 to 1,900 PSI, 1.1 to 1.3 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $100 to $250</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cars, furniture, small patios</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mid-range electric</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,000 to 2,300 PSI, 1.2 to 1.5 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $200 to $450</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Decks, siding, average driveways</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cordless battery</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies widely</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $150 to $450+ with battery</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remote jobs, boats, light work</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Homeowner gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,700 to 3,400 PSI, 2.3 to 2.6 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $300 to $600</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large driveways, heavy grime</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pro-grade gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,500 to 4,400 PSI, 3 to 4 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $700 to $1,500+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Daily or commercial use</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hot water commercial</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often $3,000 and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grease, fleets, industrial</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Light-duty electric is where most people should start and where many should stay. These machines are small, quiet, need no fuel or oil changes, and are the cheapest to own. Their weakness is flow, measured in gallons per minute, which determines how fast you can rinse dirt away. A light-duty electric will clean a two-car driveway, but it will take noticeably longer than a gas machine, and many owners add a surface cleaner to speed it up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mid-range electrics are the sweet spot for a typical suburban house. They tend to have better pumps, longer hoses, sturdier wands and often induction motors that run quieter and last longer than the universal motors in cheaper units. If you plan to clean a deck, siding and a driveway every year, this is usually the class where spending a bit more pays off in time and durability. Our <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best electric pressure washers</a> guide covers the models in this range we would actually buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas machines cost more to buy and more to keep, but they move far more water, which is what really speeds up big flat jobs. Homeowner gas units in the $300 to $600 range are adequate for occasional use. Pro-grade machines cost more because of what you cannot see: triplex plunger pumps that can be rebuilt, commercial-grade engines, and steel frames. That durability is worth paying for only if you will put real hours on the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Makes One Pressure Washer Cost More Than Another?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pump is the single biggest factor. Cheap machines use axial cam pumps that are sealed units, effectively disposable when they wear out. Mid and upper-range gas machines use better axial pumps, and pro-grade machines use triplex plunger pumps with replaceable seals and valves that can last many times longer. When you compare two machines with the same PSI rating and a large price difference, the pump is usually the reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flow and pressure ratings come next. Price climbs with both, but flow, the GPM number, is more expensive to deliver and matters more for cleaning speed. A machine rated at 3,000 PSI and 2.5 GPM will clean concrete much faster than one rated at 2,000 PSI and 1.2 GPM, even though the pressure difference looks modest. If you want the full explanation of how those two numbers interact, the site's guide on <a href="/what-psi-pressure-washer-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what PSI pressure washer you need</a> works through it job by job.
          </p>

          <p>
            On electric machines, the motor type matters. Induction motors run cooler, quieter and longer, and they typically appear on machines in the upper half of the electric price range. Universal motors are cheaper, lighter and louder, and they wear out sooner because of their brushes. On gas machines, a name-brand engine with a good parts network typically adds to the price but makes the machine easier to keep running for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Build quality and accessories make up the rest. A steel or reinforced frame, a metal wand instead of plastic, a longer non-kinking hose, quick-connect nozzles, a hose reel and an onboard detergent tank all add cost. Some of these are genuinely useful, such as a longer hose and a good set of nozzles. Others, like integrated storage for accessories you rarely use, are nice but not worth paying much extra for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, timing changes the price. Pressure washers are seasonal products, and many retailers discount them in spring promotions, around holiday weekends and at the end of the outdoor season. If you are not in a hurry, waiting for a sale can often save a meaningful chunk of the list price. Prices also drift over time, so treat every number in this guide as a typical range rather than a fixed figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Does It Cost to Run a Pressure Washer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric machines are cheap to run. A typical corded pressure washer on a standard 15-amp household circuit draws somewhere around 1.5 to 1.8 kilowatts at full load. With US residential electricity typically in the range of roughly 15 to 20 cents per kilowatt-hour, and higher in some states, an hour of continuous spraying costs somewhere around 25 to 40 cents. Most real jobs involve pauses, so the true cost per session is often less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas machines cost more per hour. A homeowner gas pressure washer with a typical small engine burns somewhere around a third of a gallon to half a gallon of gasoline per hour under load, depending on engine size and how hard it is working. At typical US gas prices, that works out to roughly one to two dollars an hour. Add fuel stabilizer for storage, engine oil changes, and in some models pump oil, and the yearly maintenance outlay is typically modest but real.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Running Costs</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 to 40 cents</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Electric, per hour of spraying</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 to 2 dollars</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Gas fuel, per hour</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">70 to 150 gal</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water used per hour</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Water is the cost people forget, and it is usually small but not zero. A light electric machine uses roughly 1.2 to 1.5 gallons per minute, which is around 70 to 90 gallons an hour. A homeowner gas machine at 2.5 GPM uses about 150 gallons an hour. Combined water and sewer rates vary widely by city, but at typical US rates that is often well under two dollars an hour. In drought areas with restrictions, check local rules before planning a big job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Detergent is the last consumable. A gallon of concentrated pressure washer detergent typically costs roughly ten to twenty-five dollars and covers several jobs, so a couple of dollars per session is typical. Do not try to save money by running household products through the machine instead; our guide on <a href="/can-you-use-dish-soap-in-a-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">using dish soap in a pressure washer</a> explains why that backfires. Over a typical homeowner year of a few sessions, the total running cost of an electric machine is often under twenty dollars.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="accessories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Accessories and Hidden Costs Should You Budget For?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common add-on is a surface cleaner, the spinning disc attachment that cleans flat concrete without stripes. Consumer models typically cost roughly $40 to $150 depending on size and build, and for anyone cleaning a driveway or patio, it is the accessory that most changes the result. It is also the one most worth matching to your machine's flow rating, because an undersized pump will not spin a large surface cleaner effectively.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hoses are the next hidden cost. The hose included with many electric machines is short and stiff, and an upgraded or extension hose typically costs roughly $30 to $80. You may also need a garden hose long enough to supply the machine, and if you have to use an extension cord with an electric washer, it must be heavy enough for the current. Our <a href="/pressure-washer-extension-cord-size/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">extension cord size guide</a> covers which gauge to buy for which length.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Worth it?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface cleaner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $40 to $150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, for driveways and patios</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Longer or upgraded hose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $30 to $80</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Turbo or rotating nozzle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $20 to $40</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">For stubborn concrete stains</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Extension wand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $25 to $60</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">For second-story siding</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Detergent, per gallon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $10 to $25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, use the right one</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pump saver or winterizer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $10 to $15</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, in cold climates</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Safety glasses, hearing protection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $15 to $40</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, especially with gas</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Maintenance is the slower hidden cost. Gas machines need oil changes, spark plugs, air filters and fuel stabilizer, and carburetors are the most common failure after storage with old fuel. Electric machines need less, but both types benefit from a pump protector product before winter storage in cold climates, where a cracked pump from frozen water is one of the most common and most avoidable repairs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage and replacement round out the true cost. A cheap electric machine that lasts a few seasons and then fails is not necessarily a bad deal if you use it twice a year, but it is a poor deal if you use it every weekend. When you compare models, think about cost per year of service, not just the sticker price, and factor in whether replacement parts like pumps and wands are available at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is It Cheaper to Rent a Pressure Washer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a single job, often yes. Home improvement stores and equipment rental centers commonly rent gas pressure washers for roughly $50 to $80 for a few hours and roughly $75 to $120 or more for a full day, with higher rates for bigger commercial machines. Add a surface cleaner and the bill climbs. If you need to clean one driveway once and have no plans to do it again, renting a powerful machine for half a day is hard to beat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The math changes quickly once you do it more than once or twice. Two or three day-long rentals cost about as much as a decent electric machine, and the rental clock adds pressure to finish before the return deadline. You also have to transport a heavy gas machine, sometimes learn an unfamiliar setup, and accept whatever condition the nozzles and hoses are in. Owning means doing small jobs whenever you notice them, which is often how surfaces stay cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rental machines do have one real advantage: power. A rental is usually a commercial-grade gas unit with more flow than most homeowners would buy. For a once-a-year blitz on a large, heavily stained concrete area, that extra flow saves time. But that same power makes it easier to damage wood, siding and soft surfaces, so if you rent for a deck or house wash, use a wide tip and keep your distance.
          </p>

          <p>
            A reasonable rule of thumb: if you expect to pressure wash fewer than once a year, rent. If you expect to do it twice a year or more, a mid-range electric machine typically pays for itself within a couple of seasons compared with renting. If you are torn between electric and gas for ownership, our <a href="/electric-vs-gas-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">electric vs gas pressure washer</a> comparison lays out which jobs justify the higher price of gas.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hire" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does a Pressure Washing Service Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hiring a professional is the most expensive option per job but the cheapest in your time. Pricing varies a lot by region, the size and condition of the surface, access and whether chemical treatments are included. As a broad guide, many companies price flat surfaces somewhere in the range of roughly 10 to 50 cents per square foot or more, and quote house washing by size and number of stories rather than by the hour.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical pro price range</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What changes it</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Driveway, two-car</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $100 to $300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Size, staining, sealing add-on</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">House wash, one story</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $250 to $500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Square footage, mildew level</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">House wash, two story</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $350 to $700+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Height, access, soft wash</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Deck cleaning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $150 to $450</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Size, wood type, brightener</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Patio or walkway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $100 to $250</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pavers, sanding, sealing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fence</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $150 to $500+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Length, both sides, condition</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            These figures are typical US ranges, and quotes in high-cost metro areas can be well above them while rural quotes can be lower. Many companies have a minimum charge for a visit, and bundling several jobs, such as house, driveway and walkway together, often brings the per-job price down. Treat the table as a sanity check on quotes, not as a price list.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are good reasons to hire out some jobs even if you own a machine. Two-story siding means ladder work with a recoiling wand, which is genuinely risky. Roofs should not be pressure washed at all, and professional soft washing with appropriate chemicals is the safer route. Delicate surfaces, such as old wood, stucco and painted brick, are easy to damage, and a pro with experience can avoid etching and furring that would cost more to fix than the cleaning itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            The break-even point is clear for routine jobs. If you would pay a pro a few hundred dollars once or twice a year to clean a driveway and deck, a mid-range electric machine typically costs about the same as one or two service visits and lasts several years with basic care. For a one-off pre-sale clean, or a two-story house you do not want to tackle, hiring makes more sense than buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fair-price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Know You're Paying a Fair Price?
          </h2>

          <p>
            When buying, compare machines by GPM, pump type and motor or engine type before you compare price. Two machines at the same price can differ a lot in flow and durability. Be skeptical of very high PSI claims on cheap electric units, since some ratings reflect peak numbers rather than what the machine sustains in use. Look at the warranty length on the pump and motor separately, as these often differ.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for sales rather than paying list price in peak season. Spring promotions, holiday weekends and end-of-season clearances are common times for discounts. Bundles with a surface cleaner or extra hose can be good value, but only if you would have bought those accessories anyway. A bundle full of nozzles you will never use is not a saving.
          </p>

          <p>
            When hiring, get at least two or three written quotes that spell out the surfaces, the square footage or scope, whether chemicals and post-treatment are included, and who is responsible for protecting plants and windows. Ask whether they use soft washing for siding and roofs, whether they carry liability insurance, and how they handle water runoff. A quote that is much lower than the rest may skip steps or insurance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about lifespan. A cheap electric machine that dies after two seasons can cost more per year than a better one that lasts five or more. How long a machine lasts depends on build quality, how often you use it and how you store it, and our guide on <a href="/how-long-do-electric-pressure-washers-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long electric pressure washers last</a> explains what wears out first. Factor that into the cost, and the cheapest option is often not the least expensive one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on PSI Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                PSI gets the headlines, but flow in gallons per minute does more of the cleaning, and pump quality decides how long the machine lasts. Two washers with the same PSI can differ enormously in speed and durability. Compare GPM, pump type and motor or engine before you compare price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Gas for Small Jobs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gas machine costs more to buy, more to maintain and is louder and heavier. If your jobs are a car, a small patio and some furniture, a mid-range electric will do them for a fraction of the cost and hassle. Gas earns its price on big concrete areas and heavy grime.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Accessories</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many buyers find the included hose short and the nozzles limited, then spend another fifty to a hundred and fifty dollars on a surface cleaner, longer hose and extension cord. Budget for the add-ons you will really use, especially a surface cleaner if you have a driveway or patio.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Renting Repeatedly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Renting is great for a one-off job, but two or three rentals cost roughly as much as owning a decent electric machine. If you find yourself renting every year, buying is usually cheaper within a couple of seasons, and you can clean small messes whenever you notice them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Winter Storage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water left in the pump can freeze and crack it, which is one of the most common and most avoidable repairs. A pump protector product costs roughly ten to fifteen dollars and takes minutes to use. On gas machines, add fuel stabilizer or run the carburetor dry before storage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good pressure washer for the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, a mid-range electric machine around 2,000 PSI and 1.2 to 1.5 GPM gives the best balance of price and capability. It handles cars, decks, siding and average driveways without the noise and upkeep of gas. If you have a large concrete area or heavy grime, a homeowner gas machine is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cheap electric pressure washer worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For light, occasional work, yes. A budget electric machine will clean cars, furniture and small patios well enough. It will be slow on concrete and may not last as long, since cheaper pumps and motors are often not repairable. If you plan to use it often, spending a bit more usually saves money over time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to rent a pressure washer for a day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically roughly $75 to $120 or more for a full day for a homeowner-grade gas machine at a home improvement store or rental center, with lower half-day rates and higher rates for commercial units. Surface cleaners and other attachments are often extra. Prices vary by region and change over time, so check locally.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do gas pressure washers cost more to maintain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Gas engines need oil changes, spark plugs, air filters and fuel stabilizer, and carburetor problems after storage are common. Electric machines need very little routine maintenance beyond flushing and winterizing. Over several years, the gap in upkeep cost is modest in dollars but noticeable in time and hassle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should I pay to have my house pressure washed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many companies quote house washing by size and number of stories, and a one-story home commonly runs somewhere around $250 to $500, with two-story homes higher. Mildew treatment, access and region all move the price. Get at least two or three written quotes that specify the method, chemicals and plant protection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth buying a pressure washer versus hiring someone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you would hire a pro more than once a year for jobs you can safely do yourself, such as a driveway or deck, buying typically pays off within a season or two. For two-story siding, roofs or delicate surfaces, hiring is often the better choice because of the risk of injury and surface damage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless pressure washers worth the price?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you need to clean where there is no outlet or no pressurized water supply, such as a boat, a remote fence or a campsite. Many cordless models can draw from a bucket. They cost more, especially once batteries are included, and deliver less sustained power than corded electric machines, so most homeowners are better served by a corded unit.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

